import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

class Solution {
    public int fizzBuzz(int n) {
        List<Integer> ns = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (i % 11 == 0 || i % 13 == 0) {
                ns.add(i);
            }
        }
        String s = ns.toString().replaceAll("[^0-9]", "");
        int ans = 0;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            ans += (c == '7') ? 1 : 0;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

This Java code does the same thing as your Python code. It creates a list of integers from 0 to n that are divisible by 11 or 13, converts the list to a string, counts the number of times the digit 7 appears in the string, and returns that count.

Please note that the `toString()` method of a List in Java returns a string representation of the list in the format `[element1, element2, element3,...]`. The `replaceAll("[^0-9]", "")` method call removes all non-digit characters from the string. The `toCharArray()` method converts the string to a character array, and the `(c == '7') ? 1 : 0` expression is a ternary operator that returns 1 if c is '7', and 0 otherwise.

